BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pulitzer-center-global-south-civil-society-microgrants-2026@artificialn
 ouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T202139Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:Deadline: Pulitzer Center: Global South Civil Society Microgrants 2
 026
DESCRIPTION:The Pulitzer Center funds civil society in the Global South to 
 build civic engagement projects around Pulitzer Center-supported journalis
 m: grassroots collectives\, community-led movements\, ARTISTS and civic co
 alitions taking published reporting to the communities it concerns. This g
 rant cycle is restricted to the Center's Climate and Environment topics: R
 AINFOREST (drivers of rainforest degradation\, the role of global markets\
 , finance and policy\, centring the rights and roles of Indigenous peoples
 )\, OCEAN (degradation of marine environments\, ocean health and the clima
 te crisis\, ocean governance\, science-backed and community-led solutions)
 \, and CLIMATE AND LABOR (the intersection of climate and labour\, includi
 ng gender\, livelihoods\, adaptation and community resilience). WHAT COUNT
 S AS A PROJECT: community engagement and organising that connects reportin
 g with audiences\; creative expressions\, explicitly including digital cam
 paigns\, public performances and creative publication formats\; public dia
 logues bringing together affected communities\, journalists\, decision-mak
 ers and academia\; and other innovative uses of the reporting that build o
 n local cultural practices. Every project must use at least one published 
 Pulitzer Center-supported reporting project meaningfully\, or involve a Pu
 litzer Center-supported journalist centrally\, and must name a clear targe
 t audience. OUT OF SCOPE: journalism and reporting projects themselves (th
 ose go to the Center's reporting grants)\, anything not built on Pulitzer 
 Center-supported reporting\, direct advocacy or lobbying\, campaigns endor
 sing political candidates or parties\, and for-profit projects. WHO CAN AP
 PLY: grassroots organisations\, community educators\, civil society organi
 sations and coalitions\, youth movements and other groups working at the i
 ntersection of journalism\, civic rights and active citizenship\, with pas
 t experience running civic engagement activities in the Global South\, pro
 ven experience on the named topics\, and the operational capacity to manag
 e a microgrant from an international organisation. APPLICATION: a project 
 description of no more than 400 words\, the target audience and desired im
 pact\, your methodology\, a timeline and budget\, the lead applicant's CV 
 with a letter of recommendation\, and\, where activities involve Indigenou
 s peoples or traditional communities\, a signed or recorded statement of c
 onsent from a community member. Applications are accepted in English\, Spa
 nish\, Portuguese\, French and Indonesian through Submittable. TIMELINE: a
 pplications 15 August to 6 September 2026\, selection announced mid-Septem
 ber 2026\, onboarding October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Pulitzer Center\nLoca
 tion: Global South\, with the call naming Africa\, Latin America and South
 east Asia. Groups based in the United States apply to the separate U.S. Ci
 vil Society Microgrant instead.\nAward: USD 2\,000 to USD 4\,000 per proje
 ct. Applicants name the amount requested and submit a draft budget. Funds 
 are transferred to the partner after the agreement is signed\; partners fi
 le a financial narrative and outcomes report at the end but are NOT requir
 ed to collect detailed receipts. No application fee.\nDuration: Up to six 
 months of implementation\, October 2026 to March 2027\, with a virtual che
 ck-in in December 2026 and closure in early March 2027.\n\nMore: https://p
 ulitzercenter.org/opportunities/reporting-grants-fellowships/global-south-
 civil-society-microgrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
URL:https://pulitzercenter.org/opportunities/reporting-grants-fellowships/g
 lobal-south-civil-society-microgrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Global South\, with the call naming Africa\, Latin America and Sou
 theast Asia. Groups based in the United States apply to the separate U.S. 
 Civil Society Microgrant instead.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:uj-artists-in-residence-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T202139Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: University of Johannesburg Artists in Residence 2027
DESCRIPTION:UJ Arts & Culture's annual residency gives artists a monthly st
 ipend and an institutional base at the University of Johannesburg for ten 
 months\, with the option of a remote rather than physical residency. Disci
 plines covered are visual and fine arts\, music\, theatre\, performance an
 d dance\, design\, film and television\, and literary arts. A virtual info
 rmation session with Pieter Jacobs\, Head of UJ Arts & Culture\, was sched
 uled for 17 August 2026 to walk applicants through the process. ELIGIBILIT
 Y: applicants must hold a relevant post-school qualification\, with prefer
 ence for master's and PhD holders\, and must not be affiliated with anothe
 r tertiary institution in South Africa. Preference has historically gone t
 o artists with African connections. APPLICATION: via the online form linke
 d from the UJ Arts & Culture residency page\; contact kmachoga@uj.ac.za or
  +27 11 559 1476 with questions. DEADLINE: 31 August 2026. No fee.\n\nOrga
 nisation: UJ Arts & Culture\, University of Johannesburg\nLocation: Johann
 esburg\, South Africa. Both physical and remote residencies can be applied
  for\, though office\, studio\, rehearsal and performance space is limited
  and not guaranteed.\nAward: Stipend of ZAR 12\,000 per month across a 10-
 month residency\, so roughly ZAR 120\,000 in total. No travel or accommoda
 tion budget on top of the stipend\, and studio or rehearsal space is limit
 ed rather than guaranteed. No application fee.\nDuration: 10 months during
  2027\n\nMore: https://arts.uj.ac.za/artists-in-residence/?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://arts.uj.ac.za/artists-in-residence/?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Johannesburg\, South Africa. Both physical and remote residencies 
 can be applied for\, though office\, studio\, rehearsal and performance sp
 ace is limited and not guaranteed.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fifm-atlas-workshops-post-production-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T202139Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Marrakech IFF: Atlas Workshops 2026 (Films in Shooting or
  Post-Production)
DESCRIPTION:The Atlas Workshops are the Marrakech International Film Festiv
 al's industry and talent-development programme\, running since 2018 and no
 w in its 9th edition. This track is for feature films already in shooting 
 or post-production. Selected teams get personalised mentoring tailored to 
 each project across scriptwriting\, production\, distribution\, editing an
 d scoring\, plus access to the festival's industry network\, and compete f
 or cash prizes decided by a dedicated jury. ELIGIBILITY: directors and pro
 ducers who are from\, or in the diaspora of\, the Arab world or the Africa
 n continent\, working on their first\, second or third feature (fiction\, 
 documentary or animation)\, with a production company attached. Open acros
 s that whole region\, not restricted to Morocco. There is a separate Proje
 cts in Development track whose deadline (6 July 2026) has already passed f
 or this edition. Deadline 31 August 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Festival Intern
 ational du Film de Marrakech (Atlas Workshops)\nLocation: Online in Octobe
 r 2026\, then onsite in Marrakech\, Morocco\, 22-26 November 2026\, alongs
 ide the Marrakech International Film Festival.\nAward: No application fee.
  Participation in the online and onsite programme is covered. Cash prizes 
 are awarded by a dedicated jury at the close of the Workshops\; the festiv
 al does not publish the prize amounts on the call page.\nDuration: Five-da
 y online programme in October 2026\, followed by a five-day onsite program
 me 22-26 November 2026.\n\nMore: https://atlasateliers.marrakech-festival.
 com/en/call-for-applications?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
URL:https://atlasateliers.marrakech-festival.com/en/call-for-applications?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online in October 2026\, then onsite in Marrakech\, Morocco\, 22-2
 6 November 2026\, alongside the Marrakech International Film Festival.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fifm-atlas-station-producers-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T202139Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Marrakech IFF: Atlas Station 2026 (Producers Track)
DESCRIPTION:Atlas Station is the Marrakech International Film Festival's pr
 ofessional development strand running alongside the Atlas Workshops\, aime
 d at building the Moroccan film industry. The producers track offers struc
 tured mentoring\, industry sessions and access to the festival's internati
 onal network during the 9th edition. ELIGIBILITY IS RESTRICTED: open to Mo
 roccan producers only\, not to international applicants. Deadline 31 Augus
 t 2026. See also the Atlas Station short film track (deadline 14 September
  2026) and the internationally open Atlas Workshops track for films in sho
 oting or post-production.\n\nOrganisation: Festival International du Film 
 de Marrakech (Atlas Workshops)\nLocation: Online in October 2026\, then on
 site in Marrakech\, Morocco\, 22-26 November 2026. Open to Moroccan produc
 ers only.\nAward: No application fee. Participation in the online and onsi
 te professional programme is covered.\nDuration: Five-day online programme
  in October 2026\, followed by a five-day onsite programme 22-26 November 
 2026.\n\nMore: https://atlasateliers.marrakech-festival.com/en/call-for-ap
 plications?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://atlasateliers.marrakech-festival.com/en/call-for-applications?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online in October 2026\, then onsite in Marrakech\, Morocco\, 22-2
 6 November 2026. Open to Moroccan producers only.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fifm-atlas-station-short-film-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T202139Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260914
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260915
SUMMARY:Deadline: Marrakech IFF: Atlas Station 2026 (Short Film Track)
DESCRIPTION:The short film strand of Atlas Station\, the Marrakech Internat
 ional Film Festival's professional development programme for the Moroccan 
 film industry\, running alongside the 9th Atlas Workshops. Selected direct
 ors receive mentoring and industry access during the online and onsite ses
 sions. ELIGIBILITY IS RESTRICTED: open to Moroccan directors with a short 
 film in shooting or post-production that has not yet premiered. Not open t
 o international applicants. Deadline 14 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: F
 estival International du Film de Marrakech (Atlas Workshops)\nLocation: On
 line in October 2026\, then onsite in Marrakech\, Morocco\, 22-26 November
  2026. Open to Moroccan directors only.\nAward: No application fee. Partic
 ipation in the online and onsite professional programme is covered.\nDurat
 ion: Five-day online programme in October 2026\, followed by a five-day on
 site programme 22-26 November 2026.\n\nMore: https://atlasateliers.marrake
 ch-festival.com/en/call-for-applications?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://atlasateliers.marrakech-festival.com/en/call-for-applications?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online in October 2026\, then onsite in Marrakech\, Morocco\, 22-2
 6 November 2026. Open to Moroccan directors only.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:gallery-1957-yaa-asantewaa-art-prize-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T202139Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Gallery 1957: Yaa Asantewaa Art Prize 2026
DESCRIPTION:Annual prize from Gallery 1957 in Accra for women artists conne
 cted to Ghana and to Senegal\, which joins the prize for the first time in
  2026. Two winners are selected\, one from each country\, and each receive
 s USD 5\,000 in cash\, a three-month residency exchange in the partner cou
 ntry with flights\, artwork transport\, accommodation and materials covere
 d\, and a resulting exhibition with the gallery. ELIGIBILITY IS GEOGRAPHIC
 ALLY RESTRICTED: applicants must be female citizens of Ghana or Senegal\, 
 aged 18 or over\, living in Ghana\, Senegal or the diaspora\, and must pre
 sent a valid national ID or passport. Not open to international applicants
  without that citizenship. Apply by email to applications@gallery1957.com 
 with an artist bio or statement of no more than 200 words and a portfolio 
 of up to six high-resolution images or videos with captions\, both as PDFs
 . Questions go to awo@gallery1957.com. Deadline 30 September 2026.\n\nOrga
 nisation: Gallery 1957\nLocation: Accra\, Ghana and Saint-Louis / Dakar\, 
 Senegal. Open to female citizens of Ghana or Senegal residing in either co
 untry or in the diaspora.\nAward: USD 5\,000 cash per winner\, two winners
  (one Ghanaian\, one Senegalese)\, plus a fully covered three-month reside
 ncy exchange: flights\, transportation of artworks\, accommodation and mat
 erials. No application fee.\nDuration: Three-month residency exchange. The
  Ghanaian winner spends one month in Saint-Louis and two months in Dakar\;
  the Senegalese winner spends three months in Accra.\n\nMore: https://www.
 gallery1957.com/the-yaa-asantewaa-art-prize/?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.gallery1957.com/the-yaa-asantewaa-art-prize/?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Accra\, Ghana and Saint-Louis / Dakar\, Senegal. Open to female ci
 tizens of Ghana or Senegal residing in either country or in the diaspora.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:connect-and-create-circulation-performing-arts-music-2026@artificialnou
 veau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T202139Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Connect and Create: Circulation of Performing Arts and Mu
 sic (Africa-Europe) - 2nd Call
DESCRIPTION:The Circulation strand of Connect and Create funds the touring 
 and dissemination of African artistic creations across Africa and Europe. 
 It is part of the Africa-Europe Partnerships for Culture programme (EUR 8M
 \, led by the Goethe-Institut and Expertise France in partnership with Ins
 titut francais\, supported by the European Union)\, and is a different str
 and from the Professional Mobilities call also on the desk. THIS CARD IS T
 HE SECOND CALL\, deadline 30 September 2026 (the first call closed 30 Nove
 mber 2025). AMOUNT: EUR 15\,000 to EUR 39\,500 per project. WHAT IS FUNDED
 : mobility costs for artistic teams\, including transport\, visas\, insura
 nce\, lodging\, per diems\, local transport\, and shipping of scenographic
  and technical elements. WHO CAN APPLY: artistic teams (performing arts co
 mpanies\, music ensembles\, collectives) based in sub-Saharan Africa\; dis
 semination structures (production houses\, artist managers\, theatres\, la
 bels\, venues) based in Africa or Europe\; and event organisers (festivals
 \, markets\, professional gatherings) in Africa or Europe. Applicants must
  be legally established in one of the 47 eligible sub-Saharan African coun
 tries\, Kenya among them\, or one of the 27 EU Member States. INDIVIDUALS 
 CANNOT APPLY. ELIGIBLE PROJECTS\, two formats: (1) TOURS\, requiring a min
 imum of 5 pre-booked dates across at least 3 different African countries\,
  or at least 3 pre-booked dates in one or more European countries\; (2) PR
 OGRAMMING WITHIN MAJOR EVENTS\, requiring a festival\, market or professio
 nal gathering in Africa or Europe to host at least 3 artistic teams from d
 ifferent African countries. TIMELINE: activities must take place between F
 ebruary and December 2027\; selection committee December 2026\, results an
 nounced January 2027. PRIORITY is given to projects that build on existing
  tandems already collaborating\, feature emerging artists\, use eco-respon
 sible practices\, and address inclusion. APPLY via the Institut francais e
 mundus portal. Questions: connectandcreate@expertisefrance.fr.\n\nOrganisa
 tion: Goethe-Institut and Expertise France\, in collaboration with Institu
 t francais (Africa-Europe Partnerships for Culture consortium)\nLocation: 
 Applicants based in one of the 47 eligible sub-Saharan African countries (
 Kenya included) or one of the 27 EU Member States. Touring in Africa and/o
 r Europe.\nAward: EUR 15\,000 to EUR 39\,500 per project\, covering travel
 \, visas\, insurance\, accommodation\, per diems\, local transport and shi
 pping of scenographic and technical elements. No application fee.\nDuratio
 n: Projects carried out between February and December 2027\n\nMore: https:
 //ifprog.emundus.fr/en/campagne-info?view=programme&cid=353&utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://ifprog.emundus.fr/en/campagne-info?view=programme&cid=353&utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Applicants based in one of the 47 eligible sub-Saharan African cou
 ntries (Kenya included) or one of the 27 EU Member States. Touring in Afri
 ca and/or Europe.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:gasworks-residency-east-africa-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T202139Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260928
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260929
SUMMARY:Deadline: Gasworks Residency 2027: Artists Based in East Africa
DESCRIPTION:Gasworks residency open call for an early-career contemporary v
 isual artist based in East Africa. The eleven-week\, fully funded residenc
 y runs at Gasworks in London from 7 April to 22 June 2027. Gasworks' resid
 ency programme supports professional development\, cultural exchange and a
 rtistic experimentation\, hosting up to twenty residencies a year\; reside
 ncies are self-led\, non-prescriptive and process-based\, with artists exp
 ected to lead on the research and production of their own work while Gaswo
 rks provides administrative\, pastoral and curatorial support during offic
 e hours. The residency includes studio visits with Gasworks staff\, extern
 al curators\, artists and educational groups\, at least two individual stu
 dio visits with other artists\, curators and arts professionals\, networki
 ng opportunities\, a dedicated residency page on the Gasworks website with
  a short video interview filmed and edited by Gasworks\, and an Open Studi
 o event and presentation of the work and research developed during the res
 idency on 12 June 2027. ELIGIBILITY: early-career contemporary visual arti
 sts based in Burundi\, the Chagos Archipelago\, Comoros\, Djibouti\, Ethio
 pia\, Eritrea\, the French Southern Territories\, Kenya\, Madagascar\, Mal
 awi\, Mauritius\, Mayotte\, Mozambique\, Reunion\, Rwanda\, Seychelles\, S
 omalia\, South Sudan\, Uganda\, the United Republic of Tanzania or Zambia 
 only. Applicants should have at least a moderate level of spoken English a
 nd feel comfortable discussing their work in English. The residency is off
 ered subject to approval of the visa application. Deadline 28 September 20
 26\, 12:00 UTC. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: Gasworks\nLocation: Gasworks\, Lo
 ndon\, United Kingdom. Applicants must be based in East Africa: Burundi\, 
 Chagos Archipelago\, Comoros\, Djibouti\, Ethiopia\, Eritrea\, French Sout
 hern Territories\, Kenya\, Madagascar\, Malawi\, Mauritius\, Mayotte\, Moz
 ambique\, Reunion\, Rwanda\, Seychelles\, Somalia\, South Sudan\, Uganda\,
  United Republic of Tanzania or Zambia.\nAward: Fully funded eleven-week r
 esidency: 24/7 access to a fully accessible private studio at Gasworks\; s
 ingle-room accommodation in Gasworks' Residency House (shared two-storey h
 ouse with three communal bathrooms and a kitchen)\; economy return flights
  and taxi transfers from the artist's home country to London (one cabin an
 d one hold bag)\; all visa costs and visa-application support\; weekly sti
 pend of GBP 175 for living expenses\; materials budget of up to GBP 800\; 
 prepaid TFL Zones 1+2 travel card for unlimited central London travel thro
 ughout the residency\; access to Gasworks' basic tools and AV equipment su
 bject to availability. No application fee.\nDuration: Eleven weeks\, 7 Apr
 il to 22 June 2027\n\nMore: https://www.gasworks.org.uk/opportunities/resi
 dency-open-call-for-artists-based-in-east-africa-2027?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.gasworks.org.uk/opportunities/residency-open-call-for-artis
 ts-based-in-east-africa-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Gasworks\, London\, United Kingdom. Applicants must be based in Ea
 st Africa: Burundi\, Chagos Archipelago\, Comoros\, Djibouti\, Ethiopia\, 
 Eritrea\, French Southern Territories\, Kenya\, Madagascar\, Malawi\, Maur
 itius\, Mayotte\, Mozambique\, Reunion\, Rwanda\, Seychelles\, Somalia\, S
 outh Sudan\, Uganda\, United Republic of Tanzania or Zambia.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:africa-no-filter-voices-for-climate-justice-2026@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260831T202139Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: Africa No Filter: Voices for Climate Justice 2026
DESCRIPTION:Voices for Climate Justice supports African storytellers making
  work about how climate finance reaches the continent as debt rather than 
 as grants. The framing of the call: African nations are being pushed furth
 er into debt through loans despite the continent's minimal contribution to
  the crisis\, with many governments spending more on debt repayment than o
 n health and climate adaptation combined. WHO IT IS FOR: journalists\, con
 tent creators\, podcasters\, vloggers\, muralists and data visualizers. WH
 AT YOU GET: a 12-month programme delivered with Comic Relief giving 15 to 
 20 storytellers training in climate justice\, data storytelling and narrat
 ive framing\, then mentorship and work with researchers and partners to de
 velop accurate\, research-based stories\, plus content amplification and g
 rants of up to USD 4\,000. ELIGIBILITY: aged 18 or over\; based in one of 
 the ten listed countries\; an active and highly engaged platform (podcast\
 , YouTube channel\, TikTok\, Facebook\, Instagram\, publication or data vi
 sualization portfolio) running for more than a year\; a combined audience 
 of 5\,000 or more followers across Instagram\, TikTok\, Facebook\, Twitter
 /X and Snapchat for content creators and data visualizers\, while journali
 sts may instead demonstrate reach through publication circulation\; conten
 t creators must be unsigned and not represented by an agent\, though journ
 alists may be affiliated with a publication provided the submitted work an
 d content rights remain their own\; a commitment to publishing at least fo
 ur pieces on gender\, debt and fossil fuels over at least six months\; and
  individual applicants only\, no organisations or agencies. Proposals must
  focus on the campaign's core themes of climate justice\, gender\, debt an
 d fossil fuels in Africa. NOT CONSIDERED: incomplete applications\, postal
  or email submissions\, hacked material\, vulgar\, racist\, sexist or homo
 phobic material\, work reinforcing stereotypes of Africa as poor\, corrupt
 \, diseased\, conflict-ridden or lacking agency\, and accounts or posts bo
 osted by paid engagement. HOW TO APPLY: prepare a pitch deck of no more th
 an 5 slides or a video of no more than 60 seconds connecting gender\, debt
  and fossil fuels in Africa\, then submit through the ANF application port
 al with links to your platforms and a short description of the audience yo
 u plan to reach. Content may be delivered in English or French. Selection 
 weighs originality of the idea\, relevance to ANF's mission of storytellin
 g beyond stereotypes\, relevance to the gender-debt-fossil fuel focus\, an
 d reach or standing on your platform or in your community. Only shortliste
 d candidates are notified.\n\nOrganisation: Africa No Filter\, in partners
 hip with Comic Relief\nLocation: Applicants must be based in Mozambique\, 
 Uganda\, Nigeria\, Angola\, Chad\, the Republic of Congo\, Tanzania\, Sout
 h Sudan\, Malawi or Zambia.\nAward: Grants of up to USD 4\,000 (the call s
 tates a range of USD 0 to 4\,000)\, alongside training\, mentorship and co
 ntent amplification. Between 15 and 20 storytellers will be supported. No 
 application fee.\nDuration: 12-month programme. Applications opened 11 Aug
 ust 2026 and are reviewed on a rolling basis\; deadline 25 September 2026 
 at 18:00 GMT. Screening 5 October\, interviews 9 October\, outcomes commun
 icated in the first week of October and grantees announced 15 October 2026
 .\n\nMore: https://africanofilter.org/what-we-do/community/voices-for-clim
 ate-justice/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://africanofilter.org/what-we-do/community/voices-for-climate-just
 ice/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Applicants must be based in Mozambique\, Uganda\, Nigeria\, Angola
 \, Chad\, the Republic of Congo\, Tanzania\, South Sudan\, Malawi or Zambi
 a.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:africa-no-filter-kekere-storytellers-fund-2026@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20260831T202139Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261231
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270101
SUMMARY:Deadline: Africa No Filter: Kekere Storytellers Fund 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Kekere Storytellers Fund pays micro-grants to emerging Afri
 can storytellers who are already creating and publishing work that offers 
 fresh and alternative perspectives on the continent\, showing an Africa th
 at is innovative\, evolving and creative rather than defined by poverty\, 
 conflict\, disease\, poor leadership and corruption. Africa No Filter desc
 ribes the fund as its way of getting funding\, which is limited and rarely
  accessed by up-and-coming storytellers\, to artists\, journalists and con
 tent creators. PREREQUISITE: applicants must first be members of Africa No
  Filter's African Narrative Collective\, so join that before applying. ELI
 GIBILITY: African\, based anywhere in Africa or the diaspora\; under 35\; 
 able to work in English or French\; a track record of ground-breaking work
  that challenges stereotypical narratives about your community\, country o
 r the continent\; at least two years of related work experience\; a minimu
 m of 5\,000 followers across Twitter\, Facebook\, TikTok\, Instagram and Y
 ouTube\; and falling into at least one of two categories\, MEDIA AND CONTE
 NT CREATORS (journalist\, blogger\, vlogger\, podcaster\, fiction and non-
 fiction writer\, photographer) or ARTISTS (poet\, musician\, spoken word p
 oet\, dancer\, painter\, sculptor\, illustrator\, animator). NOT ELIGIBLE:
  filmmakers\, who are directed to ANF's Last Mile Film Fund instead\; stor
 ytellers without evidence of narrative-shifting work\; requests to buy equ
 ipment such as cameras or laptops\; and requests to host or attend events 
 such as workshops\, festivals or conferences. WHAT ANF IS LOOKING FOR: an 
 interesting\, unique story about Africa that amplifies unheard voices\, ch
 allenges global perspectives and introduces contemporary narratives\, with
  a clear sense of what you want the reader or viewer to take away and the 
 impact you want to create locally and globally.\n\nOrganisation: Africa No
  Filter\nLocation: Africans based anywhere in Africa or the diaspora.\nAwa
 rd: Micro-grants of USD 500 to USD 3\,000. Between 12 and 14 storytellers 
 will be selected. No application fee.\nDuration: Rolling. Applications sta
 y open until the end of the year or until 12 to 14 storytellers have been 
 selected\, whichever comes first\, so apply early rather than close to the
  nominal end date.\n\nMore: https://africanofilter.org/what-we-do/communit
